---
// The homepage. Its Markdown twin for agents is src/homepage.md (served as /index.md and at the top of
// /llms-full.txt): change both together.
import "../styles/home.css";
import mark from "../assets/mark.svg?raw";
import { site, repo } from "../../site.config.mjs";

const title = "Fleetlayer: your AI agents, across every machine and account";
const description = site.summary;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={site.url + "/"} />
    <link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown" href="/index.md" title="This page as Markdown" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:url" content={site.url + "/"} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <!-- Privacy-friendly analytics by Plausible (no cookies); the docs pages get the same from astro.config.mjs. -->
    <script async src="https://pa.docspring.com/js/pa-uqEecMZ-VpxrX9c2zdVKv.js"></script>
    <script is:inline>
      window.plausible=window.plausible||function(){(plausible.q=plausible.q||[]).push(arguments)},plausible.init=plausible.init||function(i){plausible.o=i||{}};
      plausible.init()
    </script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <header class="nav">
      <div class="nav__inner">
        <a class="wordmark" href="/" aria-label="Fleetlayer home">
          <span class="wordmark__mark" set:html={mark} />
          <span>Fleetlayer</span>
        </a>
        <nav class="nav__links" aria-label="Main">
          <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Docs</a>
          <a href="/docs/guides/backups/">Guides</a>
          <a href={repo}>GitHub</a>
        </nav>
        <a class="btn btn--primary nav__cta" href="/docs/getting-started/">Get started</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main id="main">
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
        <div class="hero__copy">
          <p class="status-line"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> Source-available · early · built in public</p>
          <!-- An uptime check looks for "until you add a second machine or account" on this page: tell the homeserver
               pve session before changing the headline. -->
          <h1 id="hero-title">Claude Code is awesome, until you add a second machine or account.</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Fleetlayer connects the Claude Code and Codex sessions on your laptop, home server and VPS, under every
            account you use. They message each other, share your signed-in browser and files, and work under rules
            you set, on machines you control.
          </p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/docs/getting-started/">Install Fleetlayer</a>
            <a class="btn btn--quiet" href={repo}>Read the source on GitHub</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="term" aria-label="Example output of fleetlayer fleet">
          <figcaption class="term__bar">
            <span>fleetlayer fleet</span>
            <span class="term__tag">example</span>
          </figcaption>
<pre class="term__body"><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> fleetlayer fleet
<span class="t-node">laptop</span> <span class="t-dim">(macbook, online)</span>
  api@laptop        claude  working  <span class="t-dim">Fixed the flaky upload test.</span>
  docs@laptop       codex   idle     <span class="t-dim">Install guide rewritten.</span>
<span class="t-node">mail</span> <span class="t-dim">(home-server, online)</span>
  triage@mail       claude  idle     <span class="t-dim">3 emails need your reply.</span>
<span class="t-node">home</span> <span class="t-dim">(home-server, online)</span>
  infra@home        claude  working  <span class="t-dim">Backup check passed.</span>

<span class="t-prompt">$</span> fleetlayer msg send --to api@laptop "the staging deploy is done"
<span class="t-ok">Delivered to api@laptop</span> <span class="t-dim">(claude-code).</span></code></pre>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="pain" aria-labelledby="pain-title">
        <div class="pain__head">
          <h2 id="pain-title">What starts going wrong</h2>
          <p>
            One Claude account on one machine is easy. Add a second account, a server, or another kind of agent, and
            this starts happening:
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="pain__list">
          <li>claude.ai artifacts say “access denied” unless you’re signed in to the right account.</li>
          <li>A session on your server can’t message one on your laptop.</li>
          <li>You keep signing in and out of the Claude in Chrome extension.</li>
          <li>An agent on your server can’t use the browser on your Mac.</li>
          <li>An agent on your server can’t reach the Downloads or Screenshots folders on your Mac.</li>
          <li>Codex and Gemini agents can’t make claude.ai artifacts at all.</li>
          <li>You find out an account is out of usage when a session stops.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="solve" aria-labelledby="solve-title">
        <h2 id="solve-title">Fleetlayer solves all of this, and much more</h2>
        <ul class="solve__grid">
          <li class="fix">
            <p class="fix__was"><span class="fix__x" aria-hidden="true">✕</span> “access denied” on artifacts</p>
            <h3>Fleetlayer Pages and Apps</h3>
            <p>Claude, Codex and Gemini agents write pages and interactive apps as plain folders, and Fleetlayer hosts them behind one sign-in.</p>
            <a class="fix__link" href="/docs/guides/pages/">Set up pages <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </li>
          <li class="fix">
            <p class="fix__was"><span class="fix__x" aria-hidden="true">✕</span> sessions that can’t reach each other</p>
            <h3>Messages between any two agents</h3>
            <p>Claude or Codex, on the same machine or another one: <code>fleetlayer msg send --to api@laptop</code>.</p>
            <a class="fix__link" href="/docs/guides/day-to-day/">Send a message <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </li>
          <li class="fix">
            <p class="fix__was"><span class="fix__x" aria-hidden="true">✕</span> signing the extension in and out</p>
            <h3>Any agent can control your browser</h3>
            <p>Agents on any machine and any Claude account can use the Chrome on your Mac, signed in as you (via Playwright MCP).</p>
            <a class="fix__link" href="/docs/guides/browser/">Share your browser <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </li>
          <li class="fix">
            <p class="fix__was"><span class="fix__x" aria-hidden="true">✕</span> an account that runs out mid-task</p>
            <h3>Your own accounts, on your own machines</h3>
            <p>Year-long sign-ins for each of your Claude accounts on every machine you run, and each account’s usage visible to every session.</p>
            <a class="fix__link" href="/docs/guides/claude-accounts/">Add your accounts <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="why fit" aria-labelledby="fit-title">
        <div class="why__col">
          <h2 id="fit-title">You need this if</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>your agents run on more than one machine, or under more than one Claude account;</li>
            <li>you run Codex or other agents next to Claude Code;</li>
            <li>an agent reads email or the web, and you want it kept away from the others' rights and secrets;</li>
            <li>you want your agents, secrets and the record of what they did on machines you control.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="why__col">
          <h2>You probably don't if</h2>
          <p>
            You use one Claude account on one laptop, and Claude Code's own features cover the rest. Use those, and come
            back when you add a second machine or account. <a href="/docs/claude-code/">What Fleetlayer adds beyond Claude
            Code</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="showcase" aria-labelledby="showcase-title">
        <div class="showcase__head">
          <h2 id="showcase-title">Your whole fleet on one page.</h2>
          <p>
            The dashboard shows every machine and what it runs, every agent session and whether it's waiting on you,
            the events that arrived and which agent each reached, and the requests waiting for your approval. Live,
            behind your own sign-in, and everything on it works from the command line too.
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot">
          <picture>
            <source media="(max-width: 40rem) and (prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="/img/dashboard-phone-dark.webp" width="780" height="1688" />
            <source media="(max-width: 40rem)" srcset="/img/dashboard-phone-light.webp" width="780" height="1688" />
            <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="/img/dashboard-dark.webp" width="2880" height="1800" />
            <img src="/img/dashboard-light.webp" width="2880" height="1800" loading="eager" decoding="async"
              alt="The Fleetlayer dashboard's overview of a fleet: machines online, sessions, what's waiting on you and problems; a list of what needs you; a card per machine with its nodes, sessions and plugins; and a live feed of events." />
          </picture>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="map" aria-labelledby="map-title">
        <div class="map__head">
          <h2 id="map-title">A node on each machine, one control plane.</h2>
          <p>
            Fleetlayer attaches to herdr without taking it over, so its app and your terminals keep working as before.
            Each machine, or each Unix user, runs a small node that keeps one outbound connection to the control plane,
            so it works behind NAT with no ports to open.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ol class="map__machines" aria-label="An example fleet">
          <li class="machine">
            <p class="machine__name">Laptop</p>
            <div class="box box--manager">
              <p class="box__label">herdr</p>
              <ul class="sessions">
                <li>api</li>
                <li>docs</li>
                <li>review</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="box box--node"><p class="box__label">node</p><p>delivers messages, runs plugins</p></div>
          </li>
          <li class="machine">
            <p class="machine__name">Home server</p>
            <div class="users">
              <div class="box box--manager">
                <p class="box__label">user triage</p>
                <ul class="sessions"><li>triage</li></ul>
                <p class="box__note">reads strangers' email, so it may message only you</p>
              </div>
              <div class="box box--manager">
                <p class="box__label">user infra</p>
                <ul class="sessions"><li>infra</li><li>backups</li></ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="box box--node"><p class="box__label">a node per user</p><p>own key, own rules</p></div>
          </li>
          <li class="machine">
            <p class="machine__name">VPS or the same laptop</p>
            <div class="box box--service">
              <p class="box__label">control plane</p>
              <p>machines, sessions, messages, webhooks, access rules</p>
            </div>
            <div class="box box--service">
              <p class="box__label">pages · sign-in · Claude accounts</p>
              <p>each one a separate piece you can move</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="map__wire" aria-hidden="true"><span>one outbound connection per node</span></div>

        <dl class="legend">
          <div><dt><span class="key key--manager"></span>Session manager</dt><dd>herdr today, others to come</dd></div>
          <div><dt><span class="key key--node"></span>Node</dt><dd>one per machine or Unix user</dd></div>
          <div><dt><span class="key key--service"></span>Service</dt><dd>run it anywhere, move it later</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="does" aria-labelledby="does-title">
        <h2 id="does-title">What you get</h2>
        <ul class="does__list">
          <li>
            <h3>Every session in one list</h3>
            <p>
              Every machine and session, with a one-line summary of what each agent is doing, and a searchable record
              of what it did. <code>fleetlayer attach api@laptop</code> watches or drives its terminal from anywhere.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Agents that message each other</h3>
            <p>
              On one machine or across machines, delivered through the agent's own channel and labelled with who sent
              it. A misspelt name suggests the right one.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Webhooks that find the right agent</h3>
            <p>
              A CI failure goes to the session that wrote the branch, which already knows the code, instead of waking a
              fresh agent with no context.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Your own accounts, on your own machines</h3>
            <p>
              Each of your Claude accounts' usage, visible to every session. Year-long tokens reach each machine sealed
              to its own key. Your sign-ins stay on a machine you control.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Separate users, separate rules</h3>
            <p>
              Run several nodes and herdr instances on the same machine or VM, each under its own Unix user. The access
              policy limits whom each agent may message and what it may use.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Tools that live on one machine</h3>
            <p>
              Your signed-in browser on the laptop, used by an agent on the server: <code
                >fleetlayer mcp browser@laptop</code
              >, limited to the callers you allow.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="does__more">
          <a href="/docs/getting-started/">See how to set it up</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="secure" aria-labelledby="secure-title">
        <h2 id="secure-title">All the security, none of the pain</h2>
        <p class="secure__lede">
          Most people run Claude Code with access to everything and hope nothing goes wrong. That works until an
          agent reads a malicious email, or a stray <code>rm -rf</code> runs in the wrong folder. Fleetlayer gives you
          the protections a security team would ask for, an agent sets them up for you, and each one can be switched
          off when you don't need it.
        </p>
        <ul class="does__list">
          <li>
            <h3>Ask before the risky things</h3>
            <p>
              Access rules decide which agent may message, start or take over which session. Anything sensitive waits
              for your approval, and you can leave approvals off for the work you trust.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Everything on the record</h3>
            <p>An audit log shows who did what and when, and you can check it hasn't been altered.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Secrets stay on your machines</h3>
            <p>Tokens travel encrypted to the machine that needs them, and the control plane never stores them.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Untrusted input stays untrusted</h3>
            <p>
              Messages from an agent that reads email or the web arrive marked as outside data. That agent runs as its
              own user and can't borrow a trusted agent's rights.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>One sign-in, one sign-out</h3>
            <p>
              One sign-in for your self-hosted services, and signing out of one signs you out of all of them.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Secrets per role</h3>
            <p>
              Items from 1Password reach only the roles you grant them to, and are scrubbed from everything that leaves
              a machine.{" "}<a href="/docs/credentials/">How</a>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Agents reach only the hosts they need</h3>
            <p>
              Each role gets a list of hosts, and you approve new ones. Linux for now.{" "}
              <a href="/docs/guides/egress/">How</a>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>An admin role that's off until you start a timed window</h3>
            <p>
              The agent that can change everything has no rights until then, and is cleaned up when the window ends.{" "}
              <a href="/docs/guides/admin-window/">How</a>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="found" aria-labelledby="found-title">
        <h2 id="found-title">A foundation for your own agent workflows</h2>
        <p class="found__lede">
          Fleetlayer started inside Quietdeck. Once we saw how useful that layer was on its own, we pulled it out into
          its own project and refined it. It gives you the primitives to build your own apps and layers on top:
        </p>
        <ul class="found__prims" aria-label="Fleetlayer's primitives">
          <li>sessions at <code>tab@node</code></li>
          <li>messages</li>
          <li>webhooks and channels</li>
          <li>access rules</li>
          <li>approvals</li>
          <li>an audit log</li>
          <li>plugins in any language</li>
          <li>shared MCP tools</li>
          <li>pages and apps</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="found__case">
          <p class="found__built">Built on Fleetlayer</p>
          <h3><a href="https://quietdeck.ai/">Quietdeck</a></h3>
          <p>An agent triages your email and proposes what to do, and you approve. Quietdeck is designed to run on Fleetlayer and uses its features to:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>forward emails and notifications to the agent that owns the work through Fleetlayer’s messaging</li>
            <li>know what all your agents are doing from Fleetlayer’s session summaries, and route each message to the right agent</li>
            <li>start an isolated email triage agent on its own Unix user, connected through its own Fleetlayer node,
              with access rules that limit whom it may message</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="works" aria-labelledby="works-title">
        <h2 id="works-title">One system, many parts</h2>
        <p class="works__intro">
          Fleetlayer is the layer between your agents and the tools around them. Each part does one job and can be
          replaced.
        </p>
        <ol class="stack" aria-label="The parts of a fleet">
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Agents</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code">Claude Code</a>, <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex">Codex</a>, <a href="https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli">Gemini</a></span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Sessions</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://herdr.dev/">herdr</a></span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row stack__row--core">
            <span class="stack__role">Fleetlayer</span>
            <span class="stack__parts">cross-agent messaging, webhooks, single sign-on, access rules, approvals, audit log, remote terminals</span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row stack__row--core">
            <span class="stack__role">Fleetlayer Plugins</span>
            <span class="stack__parts">herdr, Claude Code, AI account management, session summaries, file sync, shared folders, pages and apps <span class="stack__note">(all optional)</span></span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Tools</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://executor.sh">Executor</a> for MCP servers and sign-ins to your services</span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Secrets</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://1password.com">1Password</a></span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Browser</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp">Playwright MCP</a></span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Files</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://syncthing.net">Syncthing</a>, <a href="https://obsidian.md/sync">Obsidian Sync</a>, <a href="https://github.com/vrtmrz/obsidian-livesync">Obsidian LiveSync</a>, or any file syncing solution</span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Network</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://tailscale.com">Tailscale</a>, <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/">Cloudflare Tunnel</a></span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Services</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://gatus.io">Gatus</a> for health checks, <a href="https://traefik.io">Traefik</a> for addresses</span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Backups</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="/docs/guides/backups/">restic</a></span>
          </li>
          <li class="stack__row">
            <span class="stack__role">Mobile</span>
            <span class="stack__parts"><a href="https://github.com/0cv/herdr-mobile-relay">herdr-mobile-relay</a></span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="band" aria-labelledby="band-title">
        <div class="band__inner">
          <div class="band__copy">
            <h2 id="band-title">Start small, replace any component as you go.</h2>
            <p>
              Begin with everything on your laptop. When you outgrow it, move one piece to a home server or a VPS, or
              swap it for the tool you'd rather use: your own proxy, your company's sign-in, another session manager.
              Each piece keeps its state in one folder and speaks open protocols, so moving it is a copy and a new
              address.
            </p>
            <p class="band__link"><a href="/docs/guides/laptop/">Start on your laptop</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="band__code">
            <p class="band__step">Ask your agent</p>
<pre><code>Set up Fleetlayer on this machine. The docs are at https://fleetlayer.ai/llms.txt</code></pre>
            <p class="band__step">Or run it yourself (Linux or macOS)</p>
<pre><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fleetlayer/fleetlayer/main/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
            <p class="band__step">Start everything on this machine</p>
<pre><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> fleetlayer up
<span class="t-dim">Fleetlayer is running. Status: http://fleetlayer.localhost:7680</span>
<span class="t-prompt">$</span> fleetlayer sessions</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="why" aria-labelledby="why-title">
        <div class="why__col">
          <h2 id="why-title">No vendor lock-in</h2>
          <p>
            Your own stack instead of a vendor's cloud. Unlike one big app, Fleetlayer is a few separate pieces that work
            with the tools you already use, and you can swap any of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep using Anthropic's and OpenAI's own features for as long as they serve you. When you outgrow them
            (several subscriptions, several machines, agents from more than one vendor), you give nothing up. When a
            vendor ships its own version of a feature, Fleetlayer can support it too, and you can still change models
            and harnesses whenever you like.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="why__col">
          <h2>For people who run many agents</h2>
          <p>
            Fleetlayer is for a person running many agents across several machines and accounts: personal errands, email
            triage, product work, infrastructure, background workers, each with its own machine or Unix user and its own
            permissions, all visible from your own terminal.
          </p>
          <p>
            It grows with you. A household or a team shares one system, and a company gets what it needs (single
            sign-on, teams, separate organisations, audit) when it needs it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="features" aria-labelledby="features-title">
        <h2 id="features-title">Features</h2>
        <ul class="features__list">
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/day-to-day/">Every session on every machine in one list, with what each agent is doing</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/day-to-day/">Messages between any two agents, Claude Code or Codex, on any machine</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/day-to-day/">Sessions started, resumed, answered and handed off on any machine</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/plugins/summaries/">A summary of each hour of every session</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/ci-results/">CI results to the agent that pushed, through webhooks</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/dashboard/">The dashboard: machines, sessions, events and approvals, live</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/phone/">Every machine on your phone, through one herdr relay</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/claude-accounts/">Your own Claude accounts on every machine, with each account&#x27;s usage</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/browser/">Your signed-in browser, for agents on any machine</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/shared-folders/">Your Mac&#x27;s screenshots and downloads, for agents anywhere</a></li>
            <li><a href="/docs/guides/shared-commands/">Approved commands that run on another machine</a></li>
